  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1219 PM MDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nMOUNTAIN STORMS AND WARM TEMPERATURES:\n\nAfternoon showers and thunderstorms continue today, although\ndecreasing moisture will largely restrict convective activity \nto the higher terrain. PWATs will remain around 125 to 150% of \nnormal through Wednesday, decreasing more during the end of the\nworkweek. The elevated PWATs will support daily mountain \nconvection, which also means that concerns with flash flooding \nand debris flows from localized heavier rainfall over newer burn\nscars and susceptible terrain will persist. \n\nToday's high temperatures will be around 5 to 10 degrees above\nnormal. Westward shifting high pressure will allow for\ntemperatures to gradually increase by a degree or so each day\nthrough Friday.\n\n\nDRIER CONDITIONS LATER THIS WEEK:\n\nDrier conditions at the end of the workweek, coupled with\nincreased afternoon wind gusts as a trough brings a jet streak\nand stronger pressure gradient to the area, could create\nlocalized critical fire weather conditions on Friday and\nSaturday. However, some guidance actually favors stronger\nmoisture advection on Friday and Saturday, which decreases  \nconfidence on the specifics of where relative humidity values \nmight fall below critical fire weather thresholds. \n\nThe anticipated pattern shift this weekend is forecast to drop \nhigh temperatures on Saturday and Sunday. Current guidance \nsuggests this drop could bring highs down to near normal for \nthis time of the year.",
